    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Field measurements from the Mount Abbot quadrangle, central Sierra Nevada, California, define stages of strike-slip fault development in granitic rock: (1) Propagation of a single set of opening-mode fractures (length 1 - 100 m, spacing 2 cm - 10 m, aperture >= 0.1 - 10 mm); (2) left-lateral shear and cataclasis along some of the fractures and linkage of fracture tips by oblique, opening-mode cracks (splay cracks); (3) linkage of pairs of sheared overlapping fractures by multiple splay cracks into simple faults evolving into km-scale strike-slip faults. Up to incipient Stage 3, this evolving geometry can be characterized in terms of the joint spacing as determined by the line scan method and the spatial density of those splay cracks that interconnect joints.\r\n\r\nWe attempt to relate these parametric characteristics to effective permeability, hydraulic impedance, the flow velocity spectrum, and the solute transport characteristics of the fractured granite. These fluid-flow properties are identified via numerical simulations based on the outcrop joint/fault geometries.\r\n\r\nGoverning sensitivities are: An increase by ¾ 1.5 orders of magnitude from about 10-18 to 10-16.5 m2 in the joint parallel effective permeability due to the introduction of the poorly-connected highly-conductive joints in Stage 1; a further permeability increase by up to one order of magnitude accompanying incipient linkage of single joints into groups of linked joints with a large aspect ratio in Stage 2; localization of flow into simple strike-slip faults from Stage 2 to 3.\r\n\r\nDuring development of km-scale strike-slip faults in Stage 4, the effective fault-parallel permeability in the control volume is within one order of magnitude of that of the fault zone. Shear-induced coalescence of joints into simple strike-slip faults (Stage 3) marks the onset of percolation in the fracture network, and changes the scale of the flow inhomogeneities from meters to kilometers. A transition occurs from pervasive flow in the rock bridges among joints to flow that largely bypasses macroscopically-unfractured granite: The flow-velocity spectrum broadens and chemical dispersivity decreases appreciably.",
